      "marks": 8,
      "answer": "$1+\\sqrt5-\\sqrt3\\,\\mathrm{s}$ (approximately $1.50\\,\\mathrm{s}$).",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$s_A=\\pm(30t-5t^2)$ or $s_B=\\pm5t^2$ — B1. Guidance: Use constant-acceleration equations to find displacement expressions for $A$ or $B$.\n$s_A+s_B=15$, leading to $15=30t$ and $t=0.5$ — B1. Guidance: Use $s_A+s_B=15$ to find collision time.\n$t=0.5$ leading to $v_A=\\pm25$ and $v_B=\\pm5$ — B1. Guidance: Speeds before collision.\n$t=0.5$ leading to collision height $h_A=\\pm(30(0.5)-\\frac12g(0.5)^2)=\\pm13.75$; alternatively $h_B=\\pm1.25$ — B1.\n$25(2m)-5(m)=(3m)v_1$, giving $v_1=15$; $25(m)-5(2m)=(3m)v_2$, giving $v_2=5$ — M1 for conservation of momentum in either case, using their nonzero pre-collision velocities and 3 terms; A1 for both $v$ values correct.\nParticle $C$: $-13.75=15t-5t^2$ and $-13.75=5t-5t^2$ — M1. Guidance: Use $s=ut+\\frac12at^2$ to find $t$, using either their numerical $v$ or one from a relevant momentum equation.\n$t_{C1},t_{C2}=3.74,2.23$, leading to $T=1+\\sqrt5-\\sqrt3=1.50$ — A1. Guidance: Difference $t_{C1}-t_{C2}$ from $3.736$ and $2.232$; subscripts refer to the two cases.\nAlternative final two marks: $0=15-gt_1$, $0=5-gt_2$, giving $t_1=1.5$, $t_2=0.5$ — M1; use $v=u-gt$ for time to highest point and $v^2=u^2-2gs$ for total height, giving $h_1=13.75+11.25=25$, $h_2=13.75+1.25=15$; then $25=5T_1^2$, $15=5T_2^2$, so $T_1=\\sqrt5$, $T_2=\\sqrt3$, and use $s=\\frac12gT^2$ for time to ground. Difference $=1.5+\\sqrt5-(0.5+\\sqrt3)=1+\\sqrt5-\\sqrt3=1.50$ — A1.\nTotal: 8.",
